---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../components/Byline.astro";
import DualDoors from "../components/DualDoors.astro";
import LegalNote from "../components/LegalNote.astro";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const path = "/nics-is-not-sales";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "NICS is weather", path },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="NICS is weather. It is not a sales series. | FFL Intel"
  description="Background-check counts mix permits, used guns, and denials. Treating that volume like unit sales is how a forecast goes sideways."
  path={path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.range}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3">
      Checks
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white text-balance mb-4">
      NICS is weather. It is not a sales series.
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">
      Gearfire made the case on Outdoor Wire in 2024. A POS panel is a better
      sales series than FBI weather. It is still not the ask.
    </p>
    <Byline dateLabel="August 13, 2026" read="6 min" />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.range)}
        alt="Betsy at the range"
        width="1280"
        height="720"
        class="w-full object-cover max-h-80"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif">
      <p>
        For years the trade treated NICS like a cash register with extra
        steps. A monthly FBI count went up, and a production meeting behaved
        as if units had already left the dock. The check is weather. Weather
        is not a ledger.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gearfire said so, in public, on Outdoor Wire, 6 June 2024. Kaleb
        Seymour, VP of data analytics: a gap between the volume of background
        checks and the number of firearms sold — “multiple purchases per check
        or denials, which the NICS reports do not address.” The same file
        listed permit applications, used firearm sales, and transfers. Every
        Index release since has restated it. A background check is not a
        new-gun unit. That is correct.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then they built a same-store register series and asked the trade to
        retire NICS as the production compass. Fair, as far as the register
        goes. A POS panel of more than two thousand stores — their About line
        on the 13 April 2026 file — is a better sales series than FBI weather.
        It is still a panel. It is still last month. It still cannot see a
        search that returned nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Checks are not sales. Units are not asks. A quiet NICS month can sit
        next to a busy used-gun counter. A down Index — March, minus 11.9
        percent, Outdoor Wire, 13 April — can sit next to dealer sites full of
        people typing a model that is not on the shelf. Guns.com, 12 March,
        claimed local dealer sales on their platform up 46 percent in February
        with pre-owned about 31 percent of the mix. The Index is new firearms.
        Both can be true. Stapling them is how a forecast goes sideways.
      </p>
      <p>
        The honest stack is three files, not two. Platform comparison:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gseNics} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >NICS vs firearm sales</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
    <DualDoors />
    <p class="mt-10"><LegalNote /></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
